1
00:00:00,530 --> 00:00:03,010
The only warning Herbert Riederer got…

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:05,100
was a wet shoe.

3
00:00:05,170 --> 00:00:08,710
He was twenty-four years old, a state conservation officer,

4
00:00:08,730 --> 00:00:12,150
standing on the breakwater at Montrose Harbor in Chicago.

5
00:00:12,570 --> 00:00:17,260
He'd just written a ticket to a man who was fishing without a license.

6
00:00:17,330 --> 00:00:18,260
The sun was out.

7
00:00:18,330 --> 00:00:20,480
The lake was flat.

8
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:24,170
Then he felt water coming over his foot.

9
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:26,450
He stepped up to higher ground.

10
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:28,990
Behind him, he heard a rush.

11
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:30,880
And when he turned around…

12
00:00:30,950 --> 00:00:36,010
the people who'd been standing on that pier were being washed into Lake Michigan.

13
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:39,120
Eight people drowned in Chicago that morning.

14
00:00:39,190 --> 00:00:41,120
There was no storm overhead.

15
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:42,970
There was no earthquake.

16
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:50,020
The National Weather Service still describes it as a killer wave that rose "under calm winds and a bright sunny sky."

17
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:52,170
It was a tsunami.

18
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:54,010
On a lake.

19
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:56,329
And nobody in Chicago saw it coming,

20
00:00:56,349 --> 00:01:00,110
because the thing that caused it had left two hours earlier.

21
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:04,978
It still happens on the Great Lakes more than a hundred times a year.

22
00:01:05,048 --> 00:01:07,508
Lake Michigan is called a lake.

23
00:01:07,578 --> 00:01:09,248
That's the first mistake.

24
00:01:09,318 --> 00:01:14,178
It's 307 miles long, and more than a hundred miles wide.

25
00:01:14,248 --> 00:01:18,268
Stand on the shore in Chicago and there is no other side.

26
00:01:18,338 --> 00:01:20,068
There's only a horizon.

27
00:01:20,138 --> 00:01:22,908
12 million people live along its edge.

28
00:01:22,978 --> 00:01:29,065
In Chicago, the lakefront is the city's front yard, and on a hot Saturday in June,

29
00:01:29,085 --> 00:01:29,958
it fills up.

30
00:01:30,438 --> 00:01:32,018
Swimmers on the beaches.

31
00:01:32,088 --> 00:01:33,838
Families on the grass.

32
00:01:33,908 --> 00:01:37,079
And out on the long concrete piers, the fishermen,

33
00:01:37,099 --> 00:01:39,568
because that's where the deep water is.

34
00:01:39,638 --> 00:01:46,088
Saturday, June 26, 1954, was going to be one of those days.

35
00:01:46,158 --> 00:01:49,078
It began with a storm that was only passing through.

36
00:01:49,148 --> 00:01:54,698
A line of squalls formed near La Crosse, Wisconsin, and ran southeast.

37
00:01:54,768 --> 00:01:55,778
Madison.

38
00:01:55,848 --> 00:01:56,748
Rockford.

39
00:01:56,818 --> 00:01:57,298
Milwaukee.

40
00:01:57,368 --> 00:01:59,625
At about half past seven in the morning,

41
00:01:59,645 --> 00:02:04,348
it crossed Chicago and blew out over the lake, moving at close to 60 miles an hour.

42
00:02:06,478 --> 00:02:07,618
It was fast.

43
00:02:07,688 --> 00:02:08,848
It was violent.

44
00:02:08,918 --> 00:02:10,168
And it was brief.

45
00:02:10,238 --> 00:02:14,428
Along the lakefront, people got off the piers and waited it out.

46
00:02:14,498 --> 00:02:15,398
Then it was gone.

47
00:02:15,468 --> 00:02:17,318
The sky cleared.

48
00:02:17,388 --> 00:02:18,608
The wind dropped.

49
00:02:18,678 --> 00:02:22,588
By the time the lifeguards came on duty, the lake was still.

50
00:02:22,658 --> 00:02:25,638
So people did the most natural thing in the world.

51
00:02:25,708 --> 00:02:27,418
They went back.

52
00:02:27,488 --> 00:02:28,648
Remember that.

53
00:02:28,718 --> 00:02:31,618
Everybody in this story did the sensible thing.

54
00:02:31,688 --> 00:02:33,788
They waited for the storm to pass.

55
00:02:33,858 --> 00:02:34,938
It passed.

56
00:02:35,008 --> 00:02:36,518
And two hours later…

57
00:02:36,588 --> 00:02:39,212
the lake came for them anyway.

58
00:02:39,282 --> 00:02:42,452
The first place it reached was North Avenue Beach.

59
00:02:42,522 --> 00:02:45,832
Joseph Pecararo was the lifeguard captain there.

60
00:02:45,902 --> 00:02:47,432
He was twenty-four.

61
00:02:47,502 --> 00:02:50,522
When he arrived for work, the beach was almost empty.

62
00:02:50,592 --> 00:02:53,602
Two fishermen out on the hook-shaped pier.

63
00:02:53,672 --> 00:02:58,802
A line of rescue rowboats on the sand, in front of the beach house.

64
00:02:58,872 --> 00:03:02,732
Then the lifeguards saw the water coming up toward the boats,

65
00:03:02,752 --> 00:03:04,512
and ran to pull them higher.

66
00:03:04,582 --> 00:03:06,992
Pecararo remembered it like this.

67
00:03:07,062 --> 00:03:08,362
"And when we did…

68
00:03:08,432 --> 00:03:09,692
there was a wave."

69
00:03:09,762 --> 00:03:14,422
A wall of water went straight over them and knocked them off their feet.

70
00:03:14,492 --> 00:03:16,862
And when they came up, they laughed.

71
00:03:16,932 --> 00:03:19,882
"We thought it was kind of funny," he said.

72
00:03:19,952 --> 00:03:23,782
Seconds later, a man came running across the sand.

73
00:03:23,852 --> 00:03:26,682
A fisherman had been swept off the pier.

74
00:03:26,752 --> 00:03:29,052
His name was John Jaworski.

75
00:03:29,122 --> 00:03:34,112
He'd gone fishing that morning with his son, Joseph, who was eighteen.

76
00:03:34,182 --> 00:03:38,562
The lifeguards formed a line in the rough water, and found him.

77
00:03:38,632 --> 00:03:44,408
They were bringing his body to shore when a police car pulled up beside them,

78
00:03:44,428 --> 00:03:45,612
with four words.

79
00:03:45,762 --> 00:03:49,678
"Dozens down at Montrose."

80
00:03:49,748 --> 00:03:52,448
Montrose Harbor is a few miles up the shore.

81
00:03:52,518 --> 00:03:57,518
Its breakwater was one of the best fishing spots in the city, and that morning,

82
00:03:57,538 --> 00:03:59,488
there were people all along it.

83
00:03:59,558 --> 00:04:04,568
This is where Herbert Riederer was standing, with his ticket book, and his wet shoe.

84
00:04:04,638 --> 00:04:10,198
The National Weather Service says the water at Montrose rose more than ten feet in a few minutes.

85
00:04:10,268 --> 00:04:12,818
Not a breaking wave, like at the ocean.

86
00:04:12,888 --> 00:04:14,428
A surge.

87
00:04:14,498 --> 00:04:15,648
The whole lake…

88
00:04:15,718 --> 00:04:16,368
lifting.

89
00:04:16,438 --> 00:04:20,108
Riederer never forgot what he saw when he turned around.

90
00:04:20,178 --> 00:04:23,778
Decades later, he could still describe one woman.

91
00:04:23,848 --> 00:04:28,798
He said: "She was riding the crest of this huge wave into the harbor mouth…

92
00:04:28,868 --> 00:04:31,213
then she disappeared."

93
00:04:31,283 --> 00:04:37,798
A husband and wife, Mae and Edward Gabriel, were among those later found drowned.

94
00:04:37,868 --> 00:04:39,348
She was forty-eight.

95
00:04:39,418 --> 00:04:42,068
He was forty-nine.

96
00:04:42,138 --> 00:04:45,338
Divers pulled three bodies from the harbor that morning.

97
00:04:45,408 --> 00:04:47,938
Four more were found later.

98
00:04:48,008 --> 00:04:50,768
One of them was Theodore Stempinski.

99
00:04:50,838 --> 00:04:53,798
He was the man Riederer had just ticketed.

100
00:04:53,868 --> 00:04:58,688
It seems that when the water came, he stopped to pick up his fishing gear.

101
00:04:58,758 --> 00:05:04,388
The wave struck the shore all the way from the Chicago River, north to Wilmette.

102
00:05:04,458 --> 00:05:07,308
And then, as quickly as it had come…

103
00:05:07,378 --> 00:05:08,648
it was gone.

104
00:05:08,718 --> 00:05:10,228
The lake went flat.

105
00:05:10,298 --> 00:05:12,879
The sun was still shining.

106
00:05:12,949 --> 00:05:16,809
In 1954, nobody called it a tsunami.

107
00:05:16,879 --> 00:05:18,189
There'd been no earthquake.

108
00:05:18,259 --> 00:05:20,059
No wind to speak of.

109
00:05:20,129 --> 00:05:26,414
A wall of water had come out of a calm lake, under a clear sky, killed eight people,

110
00:05:26,434 --> 00:05:27,089
and left.

111
00:05:27,159 --> 00:05:28,589
It needed a name.

112
00:05:28,659 --> 00:05:30,509
The experts had one ready.

113
00:05:30,579 --> 00:05:31,239
Seiche.

114
00:05:31,309 --> 00:05:37,029
It's spelled S, E, I, C, H, E.

115
00:05:37,099 --> 00:05:39,299
A seiche is a slosh.

116
00:05:39,369 --> 00:05:42,909
Wind pushes the water of a lake toward one end.

117
00:05:42,979 --> 00:05:48,179
When the wind lets go, the water rocks back, like water in a bathtub.

118
00:05:48,249 --> 00:05:51,119
It was the best explanation anyone had.

119
00:05:51,189 --> 00:05:54,389
For decades, that's what the disaster was called.

120
00:05:54,459 --> 00:05:55,619
The Chicago seiche.

121
00:05:55,689 --> 00:05:58,659
But a seiche is slow.

122
00:05:58,729 --> 00:06:02,629
The whole lake tilts and rocks, over hours.

123
00:06:02,699 --> 00:06:06,839
This had been one violent surge, over in minutes.

124
00:06:06,909 --> 00:06:11,316
When scientists went back to the 1954 records with modern computer models,

125
00:06:11,336 --> 00:06:13,889
they found it had been a tsunami after all.

126
00:06:15,229 --> 00:06:18,649
A kind most people have never heard of.

127
00:06:18,719 --> 00:06:22,231
Meteotsunami.

128
00:06:22,301 --> 00:06:24,141
You know what a tsunami is.

129
00:06:24,211 --> 00:06:27,651
The sea floor moves, usually in an earthquake.

130
00:06:27,721 --> 00:06:31,281
It shoves the water above it, and a wave races outward.

131
00:06:31,351 --> 00:06:35,091
A meteotsunami is the same kind of wave.

132
00:06:35,161 --> 00:06:38,011
The only difference is what does the shoving.

133
00:06:38,081 --> 00:06:38,691
Meteo…

134
00:06:38,761 --> 00:06:40,491
as in meteorology.

135
00:06:40,561 --> 00:06:43,141
It's a tsunami made by weather.

136
00:06:43,211 --> 00:06:44,701
Here's how weather does it.

137
00:06:44,771 --> 00:06:49,111
A fast line of storms carries a sudden jump in air pressure.

138
00:06:49,181 --> 00:06:54,571
In 1954, that jump crossed the lake at around 60 miles an hour.

139
00:06:54,641 --> 00:07:00,671
As it moves, it presses down on the water, and a small wave forms underneath it.

140
00:07:00,741 --> 00:07:02,151
A few inches high.

141
00:07:02,221 --> 00:07:02,621
Harmless.

142
00:07:02,691 --> 00:07:08,931
But a wave in water travels at a speed that depends on how deep the water is.

143
00:07:09,001 --> 00:07:11,301
And this is the key to everything.

144
00:07:11,371 --> 00:07:15,308
If the storm above is moving at the same speed as the wave below,

145
00:07:15,328 --> 00:07:18,351
it stays right on top of it, and it keeps pushing.

146
00:07:19,281 --> 00:07:23,861
It's like pushing a child on a swing at exactly the right moment.

147
00:07:23,931 --> 00:07:27,001
Every push adds to the last one.

148
00:07:27,071 --> 00:07:30,831
Scientists at NOAA call it a Goldilocks event.

149
00:07:30,901 --> 00:07:34,001
If the storm is too slow, nothing builds.

150
00:07:34,071 --> 00:07:36,941
If it's too fast, it leaves its wave behind.

151
00:07:37,011 --> 00:07:42,771
But a storm moving at just the right speed for that lake builds a wave that keeps growing,

152
00:07:42,791 --> 00:07:43,991
all the way across.

153
00:07:45,621 --> 00:07:48,561
Then the wave reaches the shallows.

154
00:07:48,631 --> 00:07:49,761
It slows down.

155
00:07:49,831 --> 00:07:51,381
It piles up.

156
00:07:51,451 --> 00:07:54,631
A harbor squeezes it higher still.

157
00:07:54,701 --> 00:07:57,561
A few inches, in the middle of the lake…

158
00:07:57,631 --> 00:08:01,353
becomes ten feet at the end of a pier.

159
00:08:01,423 --> 00:08:03,523
That explains the size.

160
00:08:03,593 --> 00:08:06,183
It doesn't explain the strangest part.

161
00:08:06,253 --> 00:08:11,113
Why did it hit Chicago in sunshine, two hours after the storm?

162
00:08:11,183 --> 00:08:12,483
Follow the clock.

163
00:08:12,553 --> 00:08:13,793
7:30.

164
00:08:13,863 --> 00:08:19,243
The storm crosses Chicago and heads out over the lake, toward the southeast.

165
00:08:19,313 --> 00:08:21,453
The wave is forming beneath it.

166
00:08:21,523 --> 00:08:23,433
But it's moving away from Chicago.

167
00:08:23,503 --> 00:08:25,523
8:10.

168
00:08:25,593 --> 00:08:29,582
Storm and wave arrive together at Michigan City, Indiana,

169
00:08:29,602 --> 00:08:32,043
about forty miles across the water.

170
00:08:32,113 --> 00:08:36,033
A five-foot wall of water goes over the breakwater.

171
00:08:36,103 --> 00:08:38,003
And here's the cruel part.

172
00:08:38,073 --> 00:08:41,483
The fishermen in Michigan City had already run.

173
00:08:41,553 --> 00:08:45,073
They'd seen the squall coming, and gone for higher ground.

174
00:08:45,143 --> 00:08:48,343
The storm that made the wave was also their warning.

175
00:08:48,413 --> 00:08:50,753
Then the wave hit the shore.

176
00:08:50,823 --> 00:08:52,528
And it bounced.

177
00:08:52,598 --> 00:08:56,253
It turned around, and went back the way it had come.

178
00:08:56,323 --> 00:09:00,543
Forty miles of open lake, under a sky that was now clear.

179
00:09:00,613 --> 00:09:02,953
It took an hour and twenty minutes.

180
00:09:03,023 --> 00:09:03,763
9:30.

181
00:09:03,833 --> 00:09:05,963
It reaches Chicago.

182
00:09:06,033 --> 00:09:09,143
The people on the piers were looking at a calm lake.

183
00:09:09,213 --> 00:09:11,143
The storm was long gone.

184
00:09:11,213 --> 00:09:14,583
What was about to reach them had been made by that storm…

185
00:09:14,653 --> 00:09:16,343
carried across the lake…

186
00:09:16,413 --> 00:09:18,411
and sent back.

187
00:09:18,481 --> 00:09:22,641
What happened next is the part almost nobody knows.

188
00:09:22,711 --> 00:09:24,001
Ten days later…

189
00:09:24,071 --> 00:09:25,611
it happened again.

190
00:09:25,681 --> 00:09:31,281
On July 6, 1954, a similar storm crossed Chicago.

191
00:09:31,351 --> 00:09:35,881
But this time, the city knew what a storm like that could leave behind.

192
00:09:35,951 --> 00:09:38,181
The weather service put out a warning.

193
00:09:38,251 --> 00:09:40,381
The beaches were cleared.

194
00:09:40,451 --> 00:09:41,941
And the wave came.

195
00:09:42,011 --> 00:09:45,211
Water rushed into the parking lot at Loyola Beach.

196
00:09:45,281 --> 00:09:48,496
It ran up the steps of the beach house at North Avenue,

197
00:09:48,516 --> 00:09:52,261
the same beach where the lifeguards had laughed ten days before.

198
00:09:52,831 --> 00:09:55,291
Then it raced back out.

199
00:09:55,361 --> 00:09:57,726
Nobody was hurt.

200
00:09:57,796 --> 00:10:01,931
Two waves, ten days apart, on the same stretch of shore.

201
00:10:02,001 --> 00:10:04,001
Eight dead the first time.

202
00:10:04,071 --> 00:10:05,461
None the second.

203
00:10:05,531 --> 00:10:09,711
The only difference was that somebody told people to get away from the water.

204
00:10:09,781 --> 00:10:12,081
And Chicago did one more thing.

205
00:10:12,151 --> 00:10:16,111
It fixed steel posts and cables along its breakwaters.

206
00:10:16,181 --> 00:10:17,921
Something to hold on to…

207
00:10:17,991 --> 00:10:20,652
the next time the lake rose.

208
00:10:20,722 --> 00:10:24,982
For years, 1954 was filed away as a freak.

209
00:10:25,052 --> 00:10:27,722
A once-in-a-lifetime accident.

210
00:10:27,792 --> 00:10:29,262
It was not.

211
00:10:29,332 --> 00:10:29,939
In 2016,

212
00:10:29,959 --> 00:10:36,282
scientists searched years of water-level records from all around the Great Lakes.

213
00:10:36,352 --> 00:10:41,164
They found that meteotsunamis big enough to be dangerous, a foot high or more,

214
00:10:41,184 --> 00:10:43,332
happen on average 106 times a year.

215
00:10:45,552 --> 00:10:47,792
Lake Michigan gets the most.

216
00:10:47,862 --> 00:10:51,962
Most are small, and most arrive when nobody is in the water.

217
00:10:52,032 --> 00:10:53,192
Not all of them.

218
00:10:53,262 --> 00:10:56,002
July 4, 1929.

219
00:10:56,072 --> 00:10:57,392
Grand Haven, Michigan.

220
00:10:57,462 --> 00:11:02,542
A morning thunderstorm, then a wave that swept people off the pier.

221
00:11:02,612 --> 00:11:04,922
Ten were reported killed.

222
00:11:04,992 --> 00:11:07,522
July 4, 2003.

223
00:11:07,592 --> 00:11:10,172
Warren Dunes, Michigan.

224
00:11:10,242 --> 00:11:11,712
A storm passes.

225
00:11:11,782 --> 00:11:13,202
The sun comes out.

226
00:11:13,272 --> 00:11:15,282
Families go back into the water.

227
00:11:15,352 --> 00:11:17,252
Seven swimmers drown.

228
00:11:17,322 --> 00:11:20,132
Scientists later concluded the most likely cause.

229
00:11:20,202 --> 00:11:24,359
A meteotsunami only about a foot high, and the rip currents it set off,

230
00:11:24,379 --> 00:11:27,182
which kept pulling long after the wave was gone.

231
00:11:29,852 --> 00:11:31,542
April 2018.

232
00:11:31,612 --> 00:11:33,322
Ludington, Michigan.

233
00:11:33,392 --> 00:11:38,812
Water comes over the harbor walls, and damages docks and homes along the shore.

234
00:11:38,882 --> 00:11:42,112
It was April, so almost nobody was there.

235
00:11:42,182 --> 00:11:44,147
But this time, people took pictures,

236
00:11:44,167 --> 00:11:49,162
and NOAA used them to recreate a Great Lakes meteotsunami in a computer for the first time.

237
00:11:51,462 --> 00:11:53,642
June 2025.

238
00:11:53,712 --> 00:11:54,542
Lake Superior.

239
00:11:54,612 --> 00:12:00,332
A storm sets off a meteotsunami and a seiche at once.

240
00:12:00,402 --> 00:12:05,512
People film their beaches draining, until the lake bed lies bare.

241
00:12:05,582 --> 00:12:08,642
Then the water comes back.

242
00:12:08,712 --> 00:12:15,222
One gauge records a 45-inch rise, the largest in its thirty years of records.

243
00:12:15,292 --> 00:12:16,892
Look at the deadly ones.

244
00:12:16,962 --> 00:12:18,852
Nineteen twenty-nine.

245
00:12:18,922 --> 00:12:20,592
1954.

246
00:12:20,662 --> 00:12:21,822
Two thousand three.

247
00:12:21,892 --> 00:12:24,292
Every one came the same way.

248
00:12:24,362 --> 00:12:25,412
A storm.

249
00:12:25,482 --> 00:12:26,782
Then sunshine.

250
00:12:26,852 --> 00:12:29,242
Then the water.

251
00:12:29,312 --> 00:12:31,772
So can anyone see the next one coming?

252
00:12:31,842 --> 00:12:33,272
Not yet.

253
00:12:33,342 --> 00:12:37,299
After Ludington, NOAA placed a tsunami buoy in the middle of Lake Michigan,

254
00:12:37,319 --> 00:12:40,322
based on the ones that watch for tsunamis in the Pacific.

255
00:12:42,242 --> 00:12:47,722
It's anchored to the lake floor, and it listens for exactly this kind of wave.

256
00:12:47,792 --> 00:12:52,302
Scientists hope it will one day let them warn the shore in time.

257
00:12:52,372 --> 00:12:58,022
But today, there's still no meteotsunami warning system for the Great Lakes.

258
00:12:58,092 --> 00:13:02,342
So the warning has to be the one given by a scientist at NOAA.

259
00:13:02,412 --> 00:13:07,184
In his words: "Most people are used to thinking that once the sun comes out,

260
00:13:07,204 --> 00:13:09,392
the dangers of a storm have passed.

261
00:13:10,152 --> 00:13:13,159
However, these meteotsunami waves can be deadly and destructive,

262
00:13:13,179 --> 00:13:17,132
and continue to impact the lakes for hours after the rain and thunder have stopped."

263
00:13:22,722 --> 00:13:28,082
If you're ever on one of the Great Lakes, and a fast line of storms goes through,

264
00:13:28,102 --> 00:13:29,012
remember that.

265
00:13:29,082 --> 00:13:32,912
When the sun comes back, don't go straight back onto the pier.

266
00:13:32,982 --> 00:13:34,802
Don't go straight back into the water.

267
00:13:34,872 --> 00:13:36,992
Give the lake time.

268
00:13:37,062 --> 00:13:42,062
And if you see the water suddenly rising, or suddenly pulling away from the shore…

269
00:13:42,132 --> 00:13:43,332
don't go and look.

270
00:13:43,402 --> 00:13:45,842
Go up.

271
00:13:45,912 --> 00:13:51,532
On June 26, 1954, nobody on those piers was careless.

272
00:13:51,602 --> 00:13:53,472
They waited for the storm to pass.

273
00:13:53,542 --> 00:13:56,292
They went back when the sky was blue.

274
00:13:56,362 --> 00:13:59,012
Everything they could see told them it was over.

275
00:13:59,082 --> 00:14:02,412
Herbert Riederer's only warning was a wet shoe.

276
00:14:02,482 --> 00:14:06,742
Most of the people around him didn't even get that.

277
00:14:06,812 --> 00:14:10,094
12 million people live on the shores of this lake,

278
00:14:10,114 --> 00:14:13,792
and most of them have never heard the word meteotsunami.

279
00:14:14,112 --> 00:14:19,102
If you know someone who swims in it, fishes in it, or walks its piers…

280
00:14:19,172 --> 00:14:20,852
send them this.

281
00:14:20,922 --> 00:14:23,604
And there's something else hidden in this region,

282
00:14:23,624 --> 00:14:27,242
that most of the people living right on top of it have never seen.

283
00:14:27,312 --> 00:14:29,792
It's 1,200 feet under Detroit.

284
00:14:29,862 --> 00:14:31,532
It's the size of a city.

285
00:14:31,602 --> 00:14:34,032
And trucks are driving through it right now.

286
00:14:34,102 --> 00:14:35,552
That's the next video.

287
00:14:35,622 --> 00:14:37,882
Subscribe, and I'll see you down there.
